-- title:
--   Moore–Dickson identity modulo π over a local ring
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $W$ be a commutative local ring whose maximal ideal is principal, generated by an element $\pi$ (hypothesis `hπ`: $\mathfrak m_W = (\pi)$), and assume the image of $q$ in $W$ lies in $\mathfrak m_W$. Then there exists a polynomial $E \in W[X_0,X_1][X]$ such that, in the univariate polynomial ring over $W[X_0,X_1]$ (with $X_0 =$ `MvPolynomial.X 0`, $X_1 =$ `MvPolynomial.X 1` the two multivariate variables and $X$ the univariate variable), the product over all pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b$ ranging over `Fin q`, of the linear factors $X - \bigl(a X_0 + b X_1\bigr)$, where $a,b$ are taken as natural-number images in $W[X_0,X_1]$, equals
--   $$X^{q^2} - H\,X^{q} + F^{\,q-1}X + \pi E,$$
--   where $H = \sum_{i=0}^{q} X_0^{(q-1)i}X_1^{(q-1)(q-i)}$ (the sum over $i \in \{0,\dots,q\}$, with the exponents formed using truncated subtraction of naturals) and $F = X_0X_1^{q} - X_0^{q}X_1$, both regarded as constants via `Polynomial.C`, and the last term is `Polynomial.C (MvPolynomial.C π) * E`.
--
--   This is the Moore–Dickson identity for the product of the $q^2$ $\mathbb{F}_q$-rational linear forms in two variables, lifted from characteristic $q$ to a local ring in which $q$ belongs to the principal maximal ideal $(\pi)$: the identity holds exactly modulo $\pi$, with the error absorbed into $\pi E$. Here $F$ is the Drinfeld form and $H$ the Dickson invariant; the result feeds the analysis of the initial form of the coefficient of $X^q$ on a Drinfeld chart, and is used by [`FormalGroup.IsDrinfeldBasisAdic.exists_mem_pow_isUnit_homogeneous_of_coeff_nthSeries_of_ringEquiv_drinfeldChart`](thm.html#FormalGroup.IsDrinfeldBasisAdic.exists_mem_pow_isUnit_homogeneous_of_coeff_nthSeries_of_ringEquiv_drinfeldChart).
